IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ip-nouns`

-- Antitrust Guidelines for the Licensing of Intellectual Property (2017), parts 1 to 3:
-- what the guidelines cover, the concerns, the markets, the relationships, and the
-- choice between the per se rule and the rule of reason.
--
-- AGENCY ENFORCEMENT POLICY, NOT LAW. Every result is named for what the agencies say
-- they do, with the modal the text uses ("will", "may", "would be likely to", "ordinarily
-- will treat"; fork F25). Where the text states no outcome for a case, the answer is
-- NOTHING, never FALSE.

§ `Parts 1 to 3: scope, concerns, markets, relationships, modes of analysis`

§§ `1.0 What the guidelines cover`

DECLARE `Coverage of the form of property (1.0)` IS ONE OF
    `covered by these guidelines`
    `not covered: these guidelines do not cover the antitrust treatment of trademarks`

-- 1.0 [pdf page 3]; n.1 (trademarks); n.8 (mask works, "analogous to copyright").
@ref 1.0, n.1, n.8 [pdf page 3]
GIVEN f IS A `Form of intellectual property`
GIVETH A `Coverage of the form of property (1.0)`
`1.0: whether the guidelines cover licensing of` MEANS
    CONSIDER f
    WHEN `a trademark` THEN `not covered: these guidelines do not cover the antitrust treatment of trademarks`
    OTHERWISE `covered by these guidelines`

§§ `2.1 Reach`

DECLARE `Reach of the principles (2.1)` IS ONE OF
    `the principles of these guidelines apply equally to all licensing arrangements`

-- 2.1 [pdf page 6]: when the agencies determine a sufficient nexus to the United States and
-- that comity and foreign government involvement do not preclude enforcement, the
-- principles "apply equally to all licensing arrangements". The text states no outcome
-- when either determination is not made: NOTHING.
@ref 2.1 [pdf page 6]
GIVEN d IS A `Determinations on the reach of the antitrust laws (2.1)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Reach of the principles (2.1)`
`2.1: the reach of the principles` MEANS
    IF     d's `the agencies determine that a sufficient nexus to the United States exists to apply the antitrust laws`
       AND d's `considerations of international comity and foreign government involvement do not preclude investigation or enforcement`
    THEN JUST `the principles of these guidelines apply equally to all licensing arrangements`
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `3.1 The nature of the concerns`

DECLARE `What section 3.1 says the agencies do` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies will follow the analysis set forth in sections 3.2 to 5`

-- 3.1 [pdf page 10]: when such competition may be adversely affected, the agencies "will
-- follow the analysis set forth below" (n.29: sections 3.4, 4.2). "Sections 3.2 to 5" in
-- the result's name is this encoding's gloss on "set forth below", not the text's words.
-- The caller lists the ways that appear. When none appears, 3.1 states no outcome
-- (NOTHING); what follows for a restraint with no likely anticompetitive effect is in
-- 3.4 and 4.2.
@ref 3.1 [pdf pages 9-10]; Example 1 [pdf pages 8-9]
GIVEN ways IS A LIST OF `Way a licensing arrangement may harm competition among entities in a horizontal relationship`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 3.1 says the agencies do`
`3.1: when competition among actual or potential competitors may be adversely affected` MEANS
    IF null ways
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST `the agencies will follow the analysis set forth in sections 3.2 to 5`

§§ `3.2.2 Technology markets`

DECLARE `Technology-market analysis (3.2.2)` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies may analyse the competitive effects of the arrangement in a technology market`
    `there is no need for a separate analysis of technology markets`

-- 3.2.2 [pdf page 11]: when the rights are marketed separately from the products they are
-- used in, the agencies "may analyze" the arrangement in a technology market. n.33: for IP
-- licensed "as an integral part of a marketed good", "there is no need for a separate
-- analysis of technology markets". A caller who has not stated how the rights are
-- licensed gets NOTHING (fork F24).
@ref 3.2.2, n.33 [pdf page 11]
GIVEN t IS A `Technology-market facts (3.2.2)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Technology-market analysis (3.2.2)`
`3.2.2: whether a technology market is analysed` MEANS
    CONSIDER t's `how the intellectual property is licensed`
    WHEN `the rights to the intellectual property are marketed separately from the products in which they are used`
      THEN JUST `the agencies may analyse the competitive effects of the arrangement in a technology market`
    WHEN `the intellectual property is licensed, sold or transferred as an integral part of a marketed good`
      THEN JUST `there is no need for a separate analysis of technology markets`
    WHEN `how the intellectual property is licensed is not stated`
      THEN NOTHING

-- Example 2 Discussion [pdf page 12], stating a general rule for technology markets (fork
-- F18): with no share data or other indicia of market power, and comparably efficient
-- competing technologies, the agencies "will assign each technology the same market
-- share". n.37: "comparably efficient" means they can be used to produce close
-- substitutes at comparable costs. The share is in percent points.
-- The text states no share otherwise (NOTHING), nor where the rule above does not say a
-- technology market may be analysed (NOTHING); a count that is not a whole number of at
-- least one is impossible (NOTHING). This share is not an input to the safety zone, which
-- names its own criterion for a technology market without share data (4.3).
@ref 3.2.2, Example 2 Discussion, n.37 [pdf page 12]; n.33 [pdf page 11]
GIVEN t IS A `Technology-market facts (3.2.2)`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`3.2.2: the market share the agencies will assign to each technology` MEANS
    IF     t's `how the intellectual property is licensed` EQUALS `the rights to the intellectual property are marketed separately from the products in which they are used`
       AND (NOT t's `market share data or other indicia of market power are available`)
       AND t's `the competing technologies appear comparably efficient`
       AND n AT LEAST 1
       AND FLOOR n EQUALS n
    THEN JUST (100 DIVIDED BY n)
    ELSE NOTHING
  WHERE
    n MEANS t's `the number of technologies in the market, the licensed technology included`

-- 3.2.2 [pdf page 11], 2.3 [pdf page 7]: a technology market is the licensed technology
-- and its close substitutes, so two parties are actual competitors in it when the other
-- party's technology is such a substitute. A technology that cannot be practiced without
-- the other's is blocked by it (2.3), and Example 9 says of blocking patents that they "are
-- not substitutable" and that their owners "are not in a horizontal relationship with
-- respect to those patents": so blocked wins, and the parties are not actual competitors
-- in it, whatever the caller says of substitution (fork F16). Whether either party
-- licenses its technology to others is read by no rule (Example 5: "not important").
-- The answer is always JUST; the MAYBE is kept because tests-independent.l4 reads it so.
@ref 3.2.2 [pdf page 11]; 2.3 [pdf page 7]; Example 9 [pdf page 34]
GIVEN t IS A `Two parties' technologies (3.2.2)`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`3.2.2: the parties are actual competitors in the technology market` MEANS
    IF t's `one party's technology cannot be practiced without using the other's`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST (t's `the other party controls a technology or good close enough a substitute to constrain significantly the exercise of market power with respect to the licensed technology`)

§§ `3.2.3 Research and development markets`

DECLARE `Research and development market analysis (3.2.3)` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies may analyse the impact as a competitive effect in a separate research and development market`
    `the agencies will not delineate a research and development market`

-- 3.2.3 [pdf pages 13-14]: if the arrangement may adversely affect competition to develop
-- new or improved goods or processes, the agencies "may analyze such an impact" in a
-- separate research and development market, and they "will delineate a research and
-- development market only when" the capabilities can be associated with specialized
-- assets or characteristics of specific firms. Associated, but no possible adverse effect
-- on such competition: the text states no outcome (NOTHING).
@ref 3.2.3 [pdf pages 13-14]
GIVEN r IS A `Research and development facts (3.2.3)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Research and development market analysis (3.2.3)`
`3.2.3: whether a research and development market is analysed` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF NOT r's `the capabilities to engage in the relevant research and development can be associated with specialized assets or characteristics of specific firms`
        THEN JUST `the agencies will not delineate a research and development market`
      IF r's `the arrangement may adversely affect competition to develop new or improved goods or processes`
        THEN JUST `the agencies may analyse the impact as a competitive effect in a separate research and development market`
      OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- 3.2.3 [pdf page 14]: for entities with comparable capabilities and incentives, the
-- agencies "may assign equal market shares". A permitted method, not a rule the agencies
-- must follow (contrast 3.2.2's "will assign"). Percent points.
@ref 3.2.3 [pdf page 14]
GIVEN r IS A `Research and development facts (3.2.3)`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`3.2.3: the equal market share the agencies may assign to each entity` MEANS
    IF     r's `the entities have comparable capabilities and incentives to pursue research and development that is a close substitute for that of the parties`
       AND n AT LEAST 1
       AND FLOOR n EQUALS n
    THEN JUST (100 DIVIDED BY n)
    ELSE NOTHING
  WHERE
    n MEANS r's `the number of entities in the research and development market, the parties included`

-- The count used by Example 3 and by the research and development criterion of the
-- safety zone (4.3 [pdf page 27]): entities "in addition to the parties", each with the
-- required assets or characteristics and the incentive, counted once per independent
-- controller ("independently controlled"; fork F3 in NOTES.md).
@ref 4.3 [pdf page 27]; Example 3 [pdf page 15]
GIVEN entities IS A LIST OF `Other research and development entity`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of independently controlled entities in addition to the parties that possess the required assets and the incentive` MEANS
    count (nub (map (GIVEN e YIELD e's `who ultimately controls it`) (filter (GIVEN e YIELD (NOT e's `it is controlled by a party to the licensing arrangement`) AND e's `it possesses the required specialized assets or characteristics` AND e's `it has the incentive to engage in research and development that is a close substitute for that of the parties`) entities)))

DECLARE `Example 3's screen for a research and development joint venture` IS ONE OF
    `the joint venture ordinarily would be unlikely to adversely affect competition in the relevant research and development market`
    `the agency would consider whether the joint venture would give the parties an incentive and ability collectively to reduce investment in, or otherwise to retard the pace or scope of, research and development`
    `the agency would proceed to consider efficiency justifications for the venture`

-- Example 3 [pdf pages 15-16]: with "at least four other independently controlled
-- entities" of comparable capabilities and incentives besides the parties, the venture
-- "ordinarily would be unlikely to adversely affect competition" in the research and
-- development market; with fewer than four, the agency "would consider whether" it gives
-- the parties the incentive and ability to retard research and development; on a
-- significant risk, the agency "would proceed to consider efficiency justifications".
-- The risk is the agency's finding: NOTHING while not yet made. A significant risk found
-- wins even with four or more (fork F22): the risk sentence is not conditioned on the
-- count, and "ordinarily" leaves room for the case where the agency finds the risk anyway.
-- No significant risk with fewer than four is a case the Example does not answer (NOTHING;
-- fork F21). It speaks of the research and development market only; the venture's
-- effects in other markets are assessed as well (Example 3, last paragraph), and this
-- screen does not.
@ref Example 3 [pdf pages 14-16]
GIVEN others IS A LIST OF `Other research and development entity`
      risk IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE `Example 3's screen for a research and development joint venture`
`Example 3: the screen for a research and development joint venture` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF risk EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN JUST `the agency would proceed to consider efficiency justifications for the venture`
      IF `the number of independently controlled entities in addition to the parties that possess the required assets and the incentive` others AT LEAST 4
        THEN JUST `the joint venture ordinarily would be unlikely to adversely affect competition in the relevant research and development market`
      IF risk EQUALS NOTHING
        THEN JUST `the agency would consider whether the joint venture would give the parties an incentive and ability collectively to reduce investment in, or otherwise to retard the pace or scope of, research and development`
      OTHERWISE NOTHING

§§ `3.3 Horizontal and vertical relationships`

DECLARE `Relationship in a relevant market (3.3)` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies ordinarily will treat the relationship as having a horizontal component`
    `a vertical relationship`
    `a vertical relationship, which the agencies ordinarily will treat as also having a horizontal component`
    `no horizontal or vertical component`

-- 3.3 [pdf pages 16-17]: an arrangement has a vertical component when it affects activities
-- "in a complementary relationship"; the agencies "ordinarily will treat" a relationship
-- between licensor and licensees, or between licensees, as having a horizontal component
-- when they would have been actual or potential competitors absent the license, "even if
-- a vertical relationship also exists". n.27: a potential competitor is a firm it is
-- reasonably probable would have become a competitor. A horizontal relationship "does
-- not, in itself, indicate that the arrangement is anticompetitive" (3.3 [pdf page 17]):
-- this is an aid to the analysis, not a finding. The modal goes with the horizontal
-- component only, as in the text; the vertical component is stated flatly ("has a
-- vertical component when"). The last result is this encoding's name for the case the
-- text does not discuss (fork F25).
@ref 3.3 [pdf pages 16-17]; n.27 [pdf page 10]
GIVEN p IS A `Two parties in a relevant market (3.3)`
GIVETH A `Relationship in a relevant market (3.3)`
`3.3: the relationship of the parties in the market` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF horizontal AND vertical THEN `a vertical relationship, which the agencies ordinarily will treat as also having a horizontal component`
      IF horizontal THEN `the agencies ordinarily will treat the relationship as having a horizontal component`
      IF vertical THEN `a vertical relationship`
      OTHERWISE `no horizontal or vertical component`
  WHERE
    horizontal MEANS
           p's `they would have been actual competitors in the market in the absence of the license`
        OR p's `it is reasonably probable that one would have become a competitor of the other in the market in the absence of the licensing arrangement`
    vertical MEANS p's `the arrangement affects activities of theirs in the market that are in a complementary relationship`

@ref 3.3 [pdf page 16]
GIVEN p IS A `Two parties in a relevant market (3.3)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `3.3: the relationship has a horizontal component` IF
    CONSIDER `3.3: the relationship of the parties in the market` p
    WHEN `the agencies ordinarily will treat the relationship as having a horizontal component` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a vertical relationship, which the agencies ordinarily will treat as also having a horizontal component` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `3.4 The framework: per se rule or rule of reason`

-- 3.4 [pdf page 19]: the types named as accorded per se treatment, and the open end of
-- that list.
@ref 3.4 [pdf page 19]; 5.1 [pdf page 28]
GIVEN t IS A `Type of restraint`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the type of restraint has been accorded per se treatment` IF
    CONSIDER t
    WHEN `a type of restraint not accorded per se treatment` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

DECLARE `Mode of analysis (3.4)` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies will challenge the restraint under the per se rule`
    `the agencies will apply a rule of reason analysis`

-- 3.4 [pdf page 19]: with no efficiency-enhancing integration and a type of restraint
-- accorded per se treatment, the agencies "will challenge the restraint under the per se
-- rule"; otherwise they "will apply a rule of reason analysis". 5.1 and 5.5 apply the
-- same test to horizontal restraints and to pools.
@ref 3.4 [pdf page 19]
GIVEN r IS A `Restraint in a licensing arrangement`
GIVETH A `Mode of analysis (3.4)`
`3.4: the mode of analysis` MEANS
    IF     `the type of restraint has been accorded per se treatment` (r's `the type of restraint`)
       AND (NOT r's `the restraint can be expected to contribute to an efficiency-enhancing integration of economic activity`)
    THEN `the agencies will challenge the restraint under the per se rule`
    ELSE `the agencies will apply a rule of reason analysis`

DECLARE `What section 3.4 says the agencies do` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies will challenge the restraint under the per se rule, without an elaborate inquiry into its likely competitive effect`
    `the agencies will treat the restraint as reasonable, without an elaborate analysis of market power or the justifications for it`
    `the agencies will likely challenge the restraint without an elaborate analysis of particular industry circumstances`
    `the agencies inquire into the likely competitive effects, in an inquiry meet for the case, as in part 4`

-- 3.4 [pdf pages 19-20]. Under the rule of reason: if a restraint has no likely
-- anticompetitive effects, the agencies "will treat it as reasonable"; if it facially
-- appears always or almost always to reduce output or raise prices, and is not reasonably
-- related to efficiencies, they "will likely challenge the restraint without an elaborate
-- analysis of particular industry circumstances". Otherwise the inquiry is part 4's (n.48).
-- Findings that fire both sentences contradict each other: NOTHING (fork F16). Only the
-- conclusion of no likely effect is read here; a contrary conclusion and none yet both
-- leave the inquiry to part 4 (fork F23).
@ref 3.4 [pdf pages 19-20]
GIVEN r IS A `Restraint in a licensing arrangement`
      f IS A `Findings under the rule of reason`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 3.4 says the agencies do`
`3.4: what the agencies do with the restraint` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF `3.4: the mode of analysis` r EQUALS `the agencies will challenge the restraint under the per se rule`
        THEN JUST `the agencies will challenge the restraint under the per se rule, without an elaborate inquiry into its likely competitive effect`
      IF noEffect AND quickLook THEN NOTHING
      IF noEffect THEN JUST `the agencies will treat the restraint as reasonable, without an elaborate analysis of market power or the justifications for it`
      IF quickLook THEN JUST `the agencies will likely challenge the restraint without an elaborate analysis of particular industry circumstances`
      OTHERWISE JUST `the agencies inquire into the likely competitive effects, in an inquiry meet for the case, as in part 4`
  WHERE
    noEffect MEANS f's `the agencies' conclusion on the restraint's anticompetitive effect` EQUALS `the agencies conclude that the restraint is unlikely to have an anticompetitive effect`
    quickLook MEANS
            r's `the restraint is of a kind that would always or almost always tend to reduce output or increase prices`
        AND (NOT r's `the restraint is reasonably related to efficiencies`)
